contrib/pg_bp_lru/README

pg_bp_lru -- Least Recently Used (LRU) Buffer Replacement
==========================================================

pg_bp_lru provides the classic LRU buffer replacement algorithm as a
PostgreSQL extension.  LRU is the simplest cache replacement strategy:
the page that has gone longest without being accessed is evicted first.

This extension is primarily useful as a baseline for benchmarking more
sophisticated algorithms such as ARC, CAR, LIRS, and others.  By
measuring LRU performance alongside an adaptive algorithm, you can
quantify the value that adaptation provides for a given workload.

Usage
=====

    CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_lru;
    CREATE BUFFER POOL my_lru_pool HANDLER lru_pool_handler SIZE '33554432';
    CREATE TABLE test_data (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_lru_pool');

Algorithm
=========

LRU maintains a single doubly-linked list of cached pages ordered by
access recency.  The least recently used page is at the head (LRU end)
and the most recently used page is at the tail (MRU end).

    On hit:      Move the page to the MRU end (tail).
    On miss:     No structural change (just a stat increment).
    On evict:    Remove the page from the list.
    On new page: Insert at the MRU end (tail).
    Eviction:    Scan from the LRU end (head) for the first
                 unpinned buffer.

There are no ghost lists, no frequency tracking, and no adaptive
parameters.

When to Use
===========

LRU is a good fit when you need:

  - A performance baseline to compare against adaptive algorithms
  - The simplest possible replacement strategy for debugging
  - A reference implementation for testing the buffer pool framework
  - Minimal memory overhead per buffer (~12 bytes)

Statistics
==========

Install the extension to get the pg_stat_lru view:

    SELECT * FROM pg_stat_lru;

Columns:

    name        -- buffer pool name
    oid         -- buffer pool OID
    list_size   -- number of buffers currently on the LRU list
    hits        -- total cache hits
    misses      -- total cache misses
    evictions   -- total evictions

The general-purpose pg_stat_bufferpool view also reports per-pool hit
and miss statistics.

Limitations
===========

No scan resistance:  A single sequential scan of a table larger than
the buffer pool will flush every cached page.  LRU has no mechanism to
protect frequently accessed pages from being evicted by a one-time
scan.  Adaptive algorithms like ARC and LIRS solve this problem.

No adaptation:  LRU cannot learn from workload patterns.  It treats
every access identically regardless of whether a page has been accessed
once or a thousand times.

No VACUUM integration:  Pages loaded by VACUUM are inserted at the MRU
end just like any other page.  This means VACUUM can pollute the cache.
Algorithms with VACUUM awareness (such as ARC) insert VACUUM pages at
the LRU end to minimize cache disruption.

Spinlock on every hit:  Every cache hit acquires the global lru_lock
spinlock to move the page to the MRU end.  This limits scalability on
high-core-count systems.

No prefetch integration:  LRU does not implement the prefetch_hint
callback.

Reference
=========

LRU is a classical algorithm from 1960s operating systems research.

L.A. Belady, "A study of replacement algorithms for a virtual-storage
computer", IBM Systems Journal, Vol. 5, No. 2, 1966.
